Strings in C are usually represented by a character sequence with a
terminating NUL character.  A @samp{char *}, pointer to the first byte
of this character sequence, is what gets passed around as function
argument or return value.

The major restriction of this string representation is that it cannot
handle strings that contain NUL characters: such strings will appear
shorter than they were meant to be.  In most application areas, this is
not a problem, and the @code{char *} type is well usable.

A second problem of this string representation is that
taking a substring is not cheap:
it either requires a memory allocation
or a destructive modification of the string.
The former has a runtime cost;
the latter complicates the logic of the program.
This matters for application areas that analyze text, such as parsers.

In areas where strings with embedded NUL characters need to be handled
or where taking substrings is a recurrent operation,
the common approach is to use a @code{char *ptr} pointer variable
together with a @code{size_t nbytes} variable (or an @code{idx_t nbytes}
variable, if you want to avoid problems due to integer overflow).  This
works fine in code that constructs or manipulates strings with embedded
NUL characters.  But when it comes to @emph{storing} them, for example
in an array or as key or value of a hash table, one needs a type that
combines these two fields.

@mindex string-desc
@mindex xstring-desc
@mindex string-desc-quotearg
The Gnulib modules @code{string-desc}, @code{xstring-desc}, and
@code{string-desc-quotearg} provide such a type.  We call it a
``string descriptor'' and name it @code{string_desc_t}.

The type @code{string_desc_t} is a struct that contains a pointer to the
first byte and the number of bytes of the memory region that make up the
string.  An additional terminating NUL byte, that may be present in
memory, is not included in this byte count.  This type implements the
same concept as @code{std::string_view} in C++, or the @code{String}
type in Java.

@code{string_desc_t} is a string descriptor to a string that cannot
be written to.  There is also a type @code{rw_string_desc_t}, that is
a descriptor for a writable string.
@code{rw_string_desc_t} compares to @code{string_desc_t}, like the
pointer type @samp{char *} compares to the pointer type
@samp{const char *}.

A @code{string_desc_t} or @code{rw_string_desc_t}
can be passed to a function as an argument, or
can be the return value of a function.  This is type-safe: If, by
mistake, a programmer passes a @code{string_desc_t} to a function that
expects a @code{char *} argument, or vice versa, or assigns a
@code{string_desc_t} value to a variable of type @code{char *}, or
vice versa, the compiler will report an error.

When we write @code{[rw_]string_desc_t},
it means either @code{string_desc_t} or @code{rw_string_desc_t}.

@heading Conversions (declared in @code{"string-desc.h"})

Unfortunately, @code{string_desc_t} and @code{rw_string_desc_t}
being different types, there is no implicit conversion from
@code{rw_string_desc_t} to @code{string_desc_t}.  In places
where such a conversion is desired, the (inline) function
@code{sd_readonly} needs to be called.

@deftypefun string_desc_t sd_readonly (rw_string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s})
Returns a read-only view of @var{s}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun rw_string_desc_t sd_readwrite (string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s})
Returns a read-write view of @var{s}.

@emph{Attention:} This function is as dangerous
as a cast from @code{const char *} to @code{char *}!
@end deftypefun

@heading Side-effect-free operations (declared in @code{"string-desc.h"})

@deftypefun idx_t sd_length ([rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s})
Returns the length of the string @var{s}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun char sd_char_at ([rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s}, idx_t@tie{}@var{i})
Returns the byte at index @var{i} of string @var{s}.

@var{i} must be @code{< sd_length (@var{s})}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {const char *} sd_data (string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s})
@deftypefunx {char *} sd_data (rw_string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s})
Returns a pointer to the bytes of @var{s}.

@emph{Attention:} This function is provided so that you can use
@code{memcpy} or similar low-level functions.
Use of this function breaks the @code{[rw_]string_desc_t} abstraction, though,
and should therefore be limited to a minimum.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun bool sd_is_empty ([rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s})
Returns true if @var{s} is the empty string.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun bool sd_equals ([rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{a}, [rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{b})
Returns true if @var{a} and @var{b} are equal, i.e. have the same contents.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun bool sd_startswith ([rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s}, [rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{prefix})
Returns true if @var{s} starts with @var{prefix}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun bool sd_endswith ([rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s}, [rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{suffix})
Returns true if @var{s} ends with @var{suffix}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun int sd_cmp ([rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{a}, [rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{b})
Returns > 0, == 0, or < 0
if @var{a} > @var{b}, @var{a} == @var{b}, @var{a} < @var{b}, respectively.

This uses a lexicographic ordering,
where the bytes are compared as @code{unsigned char}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun int sd_c_casecmp ([rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{a}, [rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{b})
Returns > 0, == 0, or < 0
if @var{a} > @var{b}, @var{a} == @var{b}, @var{a} < @var{b}, respectively.

Either @var{a} or @var{b} must be entirely ASCII.

This uses a lexicographic ordering,
where the bytes are compared as @code{unsigned char}, ignoring case,
in the "C" locale.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun ptrdiff_t sd_index ([rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s}, char@tie{}@var{c})
Returns the index of the first occurrence of @var{c} in @var{s},
or -1 if there is none.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun ptrdiff_t sd_last_index ([rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s}, char@tie{}@var{c})
Returns the index of the last occurrence of @var{c} in @var{s},
or -1 if there is none.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun ptrdiff_t sd_contains ([rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{haystack}, [rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{needle})
Returns the index of the first occurrence of @var{needle} in @var{haystack},
or -1 if there is none.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun string_desc_t sd_new_empty (void)
Returns an empty string.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun string_desc_t sd_new_addr (idx_t@tie{}@var{n}, const@tie{}char@tie{}*@var{addr})
@deftypefunx rw_string_desc_t sd_new_addr (idx_t@tie{}@var{n}, char@tie{}*@var{addr})
Constructs and returns a string of length @var{n}, at the given memory address.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun string_desc_t sd_from_c (const@tie{}char@tie{}*@var{s})
Returns a string that represents the C string @var{s},
of length @code{strlen (@var{s})}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun string_desc_t sd_substring (string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s}, idx_t@tie{}@var{start}, idx_t@tie{}@var{end})
@deftypefunx rw_string_desc_t sd_substring (rw_string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s}, idx_t@tie{}@var{start}, idx_t@tie{}@var{end})
Returns the substring of @var{s},
starting at offset @var{start} and ending at offset @var{end}.

@var{start} must be <= @var{end}.

The result is of length @code{@var{end} - @var{start}}.

The result must not be freed
(since its storage is part of the storage of @var{s}).
@end deftypefun

@heading Memory-allocating operations that may fail (declared in @code{"string-desc.h"})

These functions use @code{malloc}.
If you use these functions, you need to handle out-of-memory situations yourself.

@deftypefun int sd_new (rw_string_desc_t@tie{}*@var{resultp}, idx_t@tie{}@var{n})
Constructs a string of length @var{n}, with uninitialized contents.

Returns 0 if successful.
Upon error, it returns -1 with @code{errno} set.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun int sd_new_filled (rw_string_desc_t@tie{}*@var{resultp}, idx_t@tie{}@var{n}, char@tie{}@var{c})
Constructs a string of length @var{n}, filled with @var{c}.

Returns 0 if successful.
Upon error, it returns -1 with @code{errno} set.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun int sd_copy (rw_string_desc_t@tie{}*@var{resultp}, [rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s})
Constructs a copy of string @var{s}.

Returns 0 if successful.
Upon error, it returns -1 with @code{errno} set.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun int sd_concat (rw_string_desc_t@tie{}*@var{resultp}, idx_t@tie{}@var{n}, /*@tie{}[rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{string1},@tie{}*/ ...)
Constructs the concatenation of @var{n} strings.

@var{n} must be > 0.

Returns 0 if successful.
Upon error, it returns -1 with @code{errno} set.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {char *} sd_c ([rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s})
Constructs a copy of string @var{s}, as a NUL-terminated C string.

Returns it if successful.
Upon error, it returns @code{NULL} with @code{errno} set.
@end deftypefun

@heading Memory-allocating operations with out-of-memory checking (declared in @code{"xstring-desc.h"})

These functions use @code{xmalloc} and thereby handle out-of-memory situations.

@deftypefun rw_string_desc_t xsd_new (idx_t@tie{}@var{n})
Returns a string of length @var{n}, with uninitialized contents.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun rw_string_desc_t xsd_new_filled (idx_t@tie{}@var{n}, char@tie{}@var{c})
Returns a string of length @var{n}, filled with @var{c}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun rw_string_desc_t xsd_copy (string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s})
Returns a copy of string @var{s}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun rw_string_desc_t xsd_concat (idx_t@tie{}@var{n}, /*@tie{}[rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{string1},@tie{}*/ ...)
Returns the concatenation of @var{n} strings.

@var{n} must be > 0.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {char *} xsd_c ([rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s})
Constructs and returns a copy of string @var{s}, as a NUL-terminated C string.
@end deftypefun

@heading Operations with side effects (declared in @code{"string-desc.h"})

@deftypefun void sd_set_char_at (rw_string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s}, idx_t@tie{}@var{i}, char@tie{}@var{c})
Overwrites the byte at index @var{i} of string @var{s} with @var{c}.

@var{i} must be @code{< sd_length(@var{s})}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun void sd_fill (rw_string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s}, idx_t@tie{}@var{start}, idx_t@tie{}@var{end}, char@tie{}@var{c})
Fills part of @var{s},
starting at offset @var{start} and ending at offset @var{end},
with copies of @var{c}.

@var{start} must be <= @var{end}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun void sd_overwrite (rw_string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s}, idx_t@tie{}@var{start}, [rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{t})
Overwrites part of @var{s} with @var{t}, starting at offset @var{start}.

@code{@var{start} + sd_length(@var{t})} must be @code{<= sd_length(@var{s})}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun void sd_free (rw_string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s})
Frees @var{s}.
@end deftypefun

@heading Outputting a string descriptor (declared in @code{"string-desc.h"})

For outputting a string descriptor,
the @code{*printf} family of functions cannot be used directly.
A format string directive such as @code{"%.*s"} would not work:
@itemize
@item
it would stop the output at the first encountered NUL character,
@item
it would require to cast the number of bytes to @code{int}, and thus
would not work for strings longer than @code{INT_MAX} bytes.
@end itemize
@c @noindent Other format string directives don't work either, because
@c the only way to produce a NUL character in @code{*printf}'s output
@c is through a dedicated @code{%c} or @code{%lc} directive.

Therefore Gnulib offers
@itemize
@item
a function @code{sd_fwrite}
that outputs a string descriptor to a @code{FILE} stream,
@item
a function @code{sd_write}
that outputs a string descriptor to a file descriptor.
@end itemize

@deftypefun int sd_fwrite (FILE@tie{}*@var{fp}, [rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s})
Outputs @var{s} to the @code{FILE} stream @var{fp}.

Returns 0 if successful.
Upon error, it returns -1.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun int sd_write (int@tie{}@var{fd}, [rw_]string_desc_t@tie{}@var{s})
Outputs @var{s} to the file descriptor @var{fd}.

Returns 0 if successful.
Upon error, it return -1 with @code{errno} set.
@end deftypefun

@heading Outputting a string descriptor in human-readable form (declared in @code{"string-desc-quotearg.h"})

For those applications
where the NUL characters should become visible as @samp{\0},
Gnulib provides a family of @code{quotearg} based functions,
that allow to specify the escaping rules in detail.

@deftypefun size_t sd_quotearg_buffer (char@tie{}*@var{buffer}, size_t@tie{}@var{buffersize}, string_desc_t@tie{}@var{arg}, struct@tie{}quoting_options@tie{}const@tie{}*@var{options})
Places into buffer @var{buffer} (of size @var{buffersize})
a quoted version of argument @var{arg},
using @var{options} to control quoting.

If @var{options} is null, it uses the default options.

Terminates the output with a NUL character,
and returns the written size of the output, not counting the terminating NUL.

If @var{buffersize} is too small to store the output string,
it returns the value that would have been returned had @var{buffersize} been large enough.

On output, @var{buffer} might contain embedded NUL bytes
if the style of @var{options} does not use backslash escapes
and the flags of @var{options} do not request elision of NUL bytes.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {char *} sd_quotearg_alloc (string_desc_t@tie{}@var{arg}, size_t@tie{}*@var{size}, struct@tie{}quoting_options@tie{}const@tie{}*@var{options})
This function is like @code{sd_quotearg_buffer}, except that
it returns the result in a newly allocated buffer
and stores its length, excluding the terminating NUL byte, in @code{*@var{size}}.

It is the caller's responsibility to free the result.

The result might contain embedded NUL bytes
if the style of @var{options} does not use backslash escapes
and the flags of @var{options} do not request elision of NUL bytes.
@end deftypefun

The following functions use ``storage slots''.
See module @samp{quotearg} for details.

@deftypefun {char *} sd_quotearg_n (int@tie{}@var{n}, string_desc_t@tie{}@var{arg})
Uses storage slot @var{n} to return a quoted version of the string @var{arg}.

Uses the default quoting options.

The returned value points to static storage that can be reused
by the next call to this function with the same value of @var{n}.

@var{n} must be nonnegative.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {char *} sd_quotearg (string_desc_t@tie{}@var{arg})
This is equivalent to @code{sd_quotearg_n (0, @var{arg})}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {char *} sd_quotearg_n_style (int@tie{}@var{n}, enum@tie{}quoting_style@tie{}@var{s}, string_desc_t@tie{}@var{arg})
Uses style @var{s} and storage slot @var{n} to
return a quoted version of the string @var{arg}.

This is like @code{sd_quotearg_n (@var{n}, @var{arg})},
except that it uses @var{s} with no other options to specify the quoting method.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {char *} sd_quotearg_style (enum@tie{}quoting_style@tie{}@var{s}, string_desc_t@tie{}@var{arg})
This is equivalent to @code{sd_quotearg_n_style (0, @var{s}, @var{arg})}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {char *} sd_quotearg_char (string_desc_t@tie{}@var{arg}, char@tie{}@var{ch})
This is like @code{sd_quotearg (@var{arg})},
except that it also quotes any instances of @var{ch}.

See @code{set_char_quoting} for a description of acceptable @var{ch} values.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {char *} sd_quotearg_colon (string_desc_t@tie{}@var{arg})
This is equivalent to @code{sd_quotearg_char (@var{arg}, ':')}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {char *} sd_quotearg_n_custom (int@tie{}@var{n}, char@tie{}const@tie{}*@var{left_quote}, char@tie{}const@tie{}*@var{right_quote}, string_desc_t@tie{}@var{arg})
This like @code{sd_quotearg_n_style (@var{n}, @var{s}, @var{arg})} but
with @var{s} as @code{custom_quoting_style}
with @var{left_quote} as left quote and @var{right_quote} as right quote.
See @code{set_custom_quoting} for
a description of acceptable @var{left_quote} and @var{right_quote} values.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {char *} sd_quotearg_custom (char@tie{}const@tie{}*@var{left_quote}, char@tie{}const@tie{}*@var{right_quote}, string_desc_t@tie{}@var{arg})
This is equivalent to
@code{sd_quotearg_n_custom (0, @var{left_quote}, @var{right_quote}, @var{arg})}.
@end deftypefun

@heading Modules

The functionality is split across three modules as follows:
@itemize
@item
The module @code{string-desc}, under LGPL, defines the type and
elementary functions.
@item
The module @code{xstring-desc}, under GPL, defines the memory-allocating
functions with out-of-memory checking.
@item
The module @code{string-desc-quotearg}, under GPL, defines the
@code{quotearg} based functions.
@end itemize

